ion of DNA origami\n\n  * January 2012\n  * Journal of Vacuum Science and Technology B: Nanotechnology and Microelectronics 30(1):1806-\n\nDOI:10.1116/1.3676054\n\nAuthors:\n\nFaisal Shah\n\n  * University of Notre Dame\n\nKyoung Nan Kim\n\nKyoung Nan Kim\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nMarya Lieberman\n\n  * University of Notre Dame\n\nGary H. Bernstein\n\n  * University of Notre Dame\n\n## Abstract\n\nA novel way to immobilize deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) origami on a conventional substrate using hydrogen silsesquioxane (HSQ) as a functionalized platform is demonstrated. An alternative approach to quantifying roughness of the HSQ surfaces for 2D DNA origami immobilization is also introduced. Effects of oxygen plasma treatment on the surface roughness and functionalization (for DNA origami immobilization) of exposed and developed HSQ patterns are characterized. Surface root mean square roughness of electron-beam exposed HSQ with various thicknesses is investigated and optimized down to 0.2 nm for ultrathin (sub-15 nm) HSQ patterns.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCURRICULUM VITÆ OF GARY H. BERNSTEIN (https://garyhbernstein.nd.edu/assets/637341/bernstein_cv_2025_11_05_for_web_page.pdf)\nciteturn30search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11 months ago; DOI: 10.1116/1.3676054\nH. Bernstein, X.S. Hu, and W. Porod, \n“On-Chip Clocking of Nanomagnet Logic Lines and Gates,” IEEE Trans. Nanotechnol., 11(2), 273-\n286 (2012). \n116. F. Shah, K. N. Kim, M. Lieberman, and G. H. Bernstein, “Roughness Optimization of E-Beam Exposed \nHydrogen Silsesquioxane for Immobilization of DNA Origami,” J. Vac. Sci. Technol. B, 30(1), 011806-\n011806-6 (2012).  DOI: 10.1116/1.3676054 \n115. \nP. Deshlahra, W. F. Schneider, G. H. Bernstein, and E. E. Wolf, “Direct Control of Electron \nTransfer to the Surface-CO Bond on a Pt/TiO2 Catalytic Diode,” J. Am. Chem. Soc., 133(41), 16459-\n16467 (2011)