le antibodies on a portable smartphone microscope. Overall, fluorescence-enhanced antibody detection in DNA origami nanoantennas shows that fluorescence-enhanced biosensing can be expanded beyond the scope of the nucleic acids realm.\n\n## Graphical abstract\n\nImage\n\n  1. Download: Download high-res image (187KB)\n  2. Download: Download full-size image\n\n\n## Subject areas\n\nPhysical chemistry\n\nOptics\n\nNanotechnology\n\nBiochemistry\n\nLoading...\n\n## Data and code availability\n\n  * •\n\nAll raw data reported in this paper will be shared by the lead contact upon request.\n\n  * •\n\nThis paper does not report original code.\n\n  * •\n\nAny additional information required to reanalyze the data reported in this paper is available from the lead contact upon request.\n\n## References\n\n  1. Acuna et al., 2012\n\nG.P. Acuna, F.M. Moller, P. Holzmeister, S. Beater, B. Lalkens, P. Tinnefeld\n\nFluorescence enhancement at docking sites of DNA-directed self-assembled nanoantennas\n\nScience, 338 (2012), pp. 506-510, 10.1126/science.1228638\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBiochemistry. Templating a molecular tug-of-war. (https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23118181/)\nciteturn30search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 14.0 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; 2012 Nov 2;338(6107):662-5. doi: 10.1126/science.1226734.\n# Biochemistry. Templating a molecular tug-of-war.\n\nScience (New York, N.Y.)\n\nPMID: 23118181\n\nPublication date: 2012 Nov 02\n\nPublication status: ppublish\n\nJournal abbreviation: Science\n\nJournal country: United States\n\nJournal NLM unique ID: 0404511\n\nJournal linking ISSN: 0036-8075\n\nISSN: 1095-9203\n\nVolume: 338\n\nIssue: 6107\n\nPages: 626-7\n\n## Identifiers\n\n  * DOI: 10.1126/science.1230818\n  * PII: 338/6107/626\n\n## Authors\n\n  * Michael R Diehl\n\n## Affiliations\n\n  * Department of Chemistry, Rice University, Houston, TX 77030, USA. diehl@rice.edu\n\n## Languages\n\n  * eng\n\n## Publication types\n\n  * Journal Article\n  * Comment\n\n## MeSH terms\n\n  * Cytoplasmic Dyneins / metabolism\n  * DNA / chemistry\n  * DNA / metabolism\n  * Kinesins / metabolism\n  * Microtubules / metabolism\n  * Molecular Motor Proteins / metabolism\n\n## Substances\n\n  * KIF5B protein, human\n  * Molecular Motor Proteins\n  * DNA (Registry number: 9007-49-2)\n  * Cytoplasmic Dyneins (Registry number: EC 3.6.4.2)\n  * Kinesins (Registry number: EC 3.6.4.4)\n\n## Comments and corrections\n\n  * Type: CommentOn; Citation: Science. 2012 Nov 2;338(6107):662-5. doi: 10.1126/science.1226734.; PMID: 23065903--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTug-of-War in Motor Protein Ensembles Revealed with a Programmable DNA Origami Scaffold | CiNii Research (https://cir.nii.ac.jp/crid/1362262944526208128?lang=en)\nciteturn30search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 14.0 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago;   * 10.1126/science.1226734 ... To understand transport by motor ensembles, <jats:bold> Derr <jats:italic>et al.</jats:italic> </jats:bold> (p. <jats:related-article xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\" ext-link-type=\"doi\" page=\"662\" related-article-type=\"in-this-issue\" vol=\"338\" xlink:href=\"10.1126/science.1226734\">662</jats:related-article> , published online 11 October; see the Persective by <jats:bold> <jats:related-article xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\" ext-link-type=\"doi\" issue=\"6107\" page=\"626\" related-article-type=\"in-this-issue\" vol=\"338\" xlink:href=\"10.1126/science.1230818\">Diehl</jats:related-article> </jats:bold> ) used a DNA scaffold for building an artificial cargo that could be programmed to bind different numbers and types of molecular motors with defined geometry.\n\n# Tug-of-War in Motor Protein Ensembles Revealed with a Programmable DNA Origami Scaffold\n\nDOI Web Site 23 Citations\n\n  * N. D. Derr\n    \n    Department of Cell Biology, Harvard Medical School, Boston, MA 02115, USA.\n\n  * B. S. Goodman\n    \n    Department of Cell Biology, Harvard Medical School, Boston, MA 02115, USA.\n\n  * R. Jungmann\n    \n    Department of Systems Biology, Harvard Medical School, Boston, MA 02115, USA.\n\n  * A. E. Leschziner\n    \n    Department of Molecular and Cellular Biology, Harvard University, Cambridge, MA 02138, USA.\n\n  * W. M. Shih\n    \n    Dana-Farber Cancer Institute, Boston, MA 02115, USA.\n\n  * S. L. Reck-Peterson\n    \n    Department of Cell Biology, Harvard Medical School, Boston, MA 02115, USA.\n\n## Bibliographic Information\n\nPublished\n    2012-11-02\n\nDOI\n    \n\n  * 10.1126/science.1226734\n\nPublisher\n    American Association for the Advancement of Science (AAAS)\n\n## Search this article\n\n## Description\n\n<jats:title>Push Me, Release, Pull You</jats:title> <jats:p> In eukaryotic cells, nearly all long-distance transport of cargos is carried out by the microtubule-based motors kinesin and dynein. These opposite-polarity motors move cargos bidirectionally so that they reach their cellular destinations with spatial and temporal specificity. To understand transport by motor ensembles, <jats:bold> Derr <jats:italic>et al.</jats:italic> </jats:bold> (p. <jats:related-article xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\" ext-link-type=\"doi\" page=\"662\" related-article-type=\"in-this-issue\" vol=\"338\" xlink:href=\"10.1126/science.1226734\">662</jats:related-article> , published online 11 October; see the Persective by <jats:bold> <jats:related-article xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\" ext-link-type=\"doi\" issue=\"6107\" page=\"626\" related-article-type=\"in-this-issue\" vol=\"338\" xlink:href=\"10.1126/science.1230818\">Diehl</jats:related-article> </jats:bold> ) used a DNA scaffold for building an artificial cargo that could be programmed to bind different numbers and types of molecular motors with defined geometry. A cargo with multiple copies of the same motor was transported with minimal interference, suggesting that similar-polarity motors can coordinate without the need for additional cellular factors. However, ensembles of opposite-polarity motors frequently engaged in a sort of “tug of war,” which could only be resolved by releasing one motor from the microtubule track. Thus, within the cell, it is likely that regulation is required for bidirectional transport. </jats:p>\n\n## Journal\n\n  * Science\n    \n\nScience 338 (6107), 662-665, 2012-11-02\n\nAmerican Association for the Advancement of Science (AAAS)\n\n## Details 詳細情報について\n\n  * CRID\n    1362262944526208128\n  * DOI\n    10.1126/science.1226734\n  * ISSN\n    10959203\n    00368075\n  * Web Site\n    https://www.science.org/doi/pdf/10.1126/science.1226734\n  * Data Source\n    --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEfficient Production of Single-Stranded Phage DNA as Scaffolds for DNA Origami (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4532261/)\nciteturn30search8 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11.4 years ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Science Year: 2012 , Volume: 338 Issue: 6107 First page: 662 –Last page: 665 Identifier: [pub-id-type: doi] 10.1126/science.1226734 .\nAngew. Chem., Int. Ed. Year: 2012 , Volume: 51 Issue: 20 First page: 4864 –Last page: 4867 Identifier: [pub-id-type: doi] 10.1002/anie.201200688 .Identifier: [pub-id-type: pmid] 22489067\n\n[publication-type: journal] [name-style: western] Langecker M. ; [name-style: western] Arnaut V. ; [name-style: western] Martin T. G. ; [name-style: western] List J. ; [name-style: western] Renner S. ; [name-style: western] Mayer M. ; [name-style: western] Dietz H. ; [name-style: western] Simmel F. C. Science Year: 2012 , Volume: 338 Issue: 6109 First page: 932 –Last page: 936 Identifier: [pub-id-type: doi] 10.1126/science.1225624 .Identifier: [pub-id-type: pmid] 23161995 Identifier: [pub-id-type: pmcid] PMC3716461\n\n[publication-type: journal] [name-style: western] Derr N. D. ; [name-style: western] Goodm